    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "70a5d09a-712b-510c-9ffb-0c6cd118ee64",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(M)=0$\n- **II.** $det(M)=-2$\n- **III.** $M$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+(-1)(0+0)+(-2)(0-1) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(M)=2\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "cbc34eb5-4ecc-5695-be14-c9a885391db3",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
