    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "850f91f8-8f13-50a7-b9e1-da2ecfedb2b5",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(S)=-72$\n- **II.** $S$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(S)=27$\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(0-9)+(-3)(0-12)+(-2)(0+0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-9)+(-3)\\cdot (-12)+(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-36+36 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, none of the statements are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "12b963bc-076d-54fb-92a3-4376cc4b7cd3",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
