    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ca0345e5-5994-5127-a63c-a26e46d637cb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $A$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(A)=5$\n- **III.** $A$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(-9+6)+2(-12+9)+(-2)(8-9) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =9-6+2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=5\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c16304a3-aa4b-57c3-8b36-1dc92ba68a1e",
