    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c989b75e-3e72-5959-9e21-fc6148256063",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $E$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(E)=0$\n- **II.** $E$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(E)=-1$\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(E) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|+0|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+1(0+0)+0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(E)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
